# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Never commit the filled version.

# Neon Postgres — "Connection string" from the Neon project dashboard.
# Keep ?sslmode=require. The pooled host is the one ending in -pooler.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/DB?sslmode=require"

# Anthropic — console.anthropic.com → API keys. Server-side only.
# No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix: that would ship the key to the browser.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."

# Auth.js — generate with: npx auth secret
AUTH_SECRET=""

# GitHub OAuth app (github.com/settings/developers)
# Callback URL: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/github
AUTH_GITHUB_ID=""
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=""

# Comma-separated allowlist. Everything in the app is behind auth, so an
# address that is not on this list cannot read either — there is no public view.
#
# Matched against the email GitHub returns for the signed-in account, which is
# whatever that person set as their primary address. It is not necessarily
# their work address, so each entry has to be confirmed with the person rather
# than guessed, or they get a sign-in that fails with no useful explanation.
#
# To add someone: append their address and redeploy. Note the list is
# all-or-nothing — every entry gets full read and write.
#
#   ALLOWED_EMAILS="davidjoan53@gmail.com,someone@example.com"
#
# Pending, uncomment when confirmed:
#   - girlfriend — no GitHub account yet
#   - nicolas@cloudnonic.com — his WORK address. Confirm it is the one on his
#     GitHub before relying on it: the entry above was joan@cloudnonic.com for
#     the same reason and was wrong, because that GitHub account signs in with
#     a personal Gmail. A work address is a reasonable guess and a bad default.
ALLOWED_EMAILS="davidjoan53@gmail.com"
